Below is the analysis and the minimal patch to prevent Field from mutating the user‐passed options.values object.

1) Bug scenario and root cause  
   - In your demo you pass an external object into Field:  
       const externalValues = { preExisting: 'keep me' };  
       this.field = new Field(this, { values: externalValues });  
   - Field’s constructor does  
       this.values = options.values || {};  
     and later on init/setValue it writes directly into this.values.  
   - Because this.values is just a reference to your original externalValues, every call to init/setValue mutates the external object.  

2) Fix  
   - Clone options.values up front so that Field never writes back into the original.  
   - Change the assignment  
       this.values = options.values || {};  
     into a shallow‐clone:  
       this.values = options.values ? Object.assign({}, options.values) : {};  

Below is the *SEARCH/REPLACE* patch. It preserves the original comments and code context, and introduces the clone only when options.values is provided.

```javascript
### src/field/index.js
